WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.890
- Welcome to the show (laughs).

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:06.340
Well, this morning I was
thinking about our guest today,

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.030
Gwyneth Paltrow, and I am so excited

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:12.110
to talk to her about co-parenting.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.980
And I've learned one of the
most important life lessons

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.500
for my tiny existence

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.880
which is I cannot control things.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.500
Because so much of the time,

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.410
you know, I just don't
get that luxury of things

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.820
being exactly the way
I would like them to be

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.820
and I have learned that it
is my reaction I can control.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.500
And we're gonna talk about
it when you have to co-parent

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.550
and figuring that out,

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.530
not only is it an art but
it is within our control

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.850
of how we deal with it with our kids.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:43.370
And all I ever wanna do
is make my kids feel safe.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.620
And again, we can't control everything

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.570
but we can have a true part
in how I conduct myself

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:49.790
within our family.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:51.550
And I can't wait to talk about it

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.420
because it's such a big part of my world

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:56.540
and my kids really are my priority.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.770
Another thing I couldn't
control this weekend

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.340
was I took Frankie to the aquarium

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.420
and we drove an hour
and we watched the river

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.420
out our car windows and I
hugged the curves in my minivan,

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.210
through the lanes like I
was coloring in a book.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:12.320
It was Sunday.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.420
And when we finally got there,

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.150
unfortunately I didn't know
you had to make reservations

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.680
and we were not able to get
in because rules are rules.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.810
And that's the lesson I
tried to teach Frankie.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.790
I certainly didn't wanna
teach her the wrong thing

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:26.520
in that moment.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.220
But then, of course, I asked the lady

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.699
if I gave her some cash
could she look the other way.

00:01:30.699 --> 00:01:31.532
(audience laughs)

00:01:31.532 --> 00:01:35.197
And she said, "Ma'am, you
can make reservations online

00:01:35.197 --> 00:01:36.200
"and book your tickets,"

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.380
with an unmovable tone.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.407
And I said, "Oh, God, please no.

00:01:39.407 --> 00:01:40.847
"I'm not a payer offer normally,

00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:42.837
"I just thought since you
were closing soon anyway

00:01:42.837 --> 00:01:44.597
"and I just think your
bosses should actually know

00:01:44.597 --> 00:01:46.257
"what an amazing person you are

00:01:46.257 --> 00:01:48.233
"that you will simply
not break the rules."

00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:49.700
(audience laughs)

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.440
But then I was curious,
has anyone else ever tried

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.647
to find a clever way in
when like the signs say,

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.460
"No entry," so to speak.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:00.170
I love receiving a no after
I've tried everything.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.030
And if you have a story, without
breaking the law of course,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.510
if you do have one I wanna hear it, okay?

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:07.820
Have ya tried to figure out

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:11.070
how to get your kid into a sold
out aquarium type of thing?

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.930
Go to Twitter and send us your story

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.300
and use the hashtag,
hashtag rule bending parent.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.590
So good news, Frankie learned that things

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.120
don't always go as planned

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.270
and things aren't always
within our control

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.350
and we went to Nathan's Hotdogs

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.570
and I found one open carnival game

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:29.550
and we played all the money
that woman wouldn't take.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:30.710
And good for her.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.850
And we drove home happy and life was good.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.550
And when you know your
kids are truly happy,

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.110
not because they got what they wanted

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:41.000
but because Plan B was just as good, well,

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.360
that was my morning share.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.560
And now let's get into some
important and awesome things

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.760
taking place in and around our world.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.234
It's time for Drew's News.

00:02:50.234 --> 00:02:51.433
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:02:51.433 --> 00:02:52.303
(car squealing)

00:02:52.303 --> 00:02:53.943
(cat meowing)

00:02:53.943 --> 00:02:55.974
(upbeat dramatic music)

00:02:55.974 --> 00:02:58.447
Sir! Ma'am! Guy!

00:02:58.447 --> 00:03:00.973
Ma'am, I asked you a question.

00:03:03.023 --> 00:03:04.343
Tell me your story!

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.303
It's Drew's News.

00:03:10.938 --> 00:03:12.820
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.830
Do you ever feel overwhelmed

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.540
by the tidal wave of news out there?

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.370
Well, relax. We've got you covered.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.850
We sift through it all
to find a few nuggets

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:23.890
to start your day.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:24.723
And here we go.

00:03:24.723 --> 00:03:26.623
Let's see what made the papers.

00:03:26.623 --> 00:03:28.159
(audience applauds)

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:28.992
(Drew clears through loudly)

00:03:28.992 --> 00:03:31.239
Excuse me, frog in throat. Hang on.

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:36.239
(clears throat loudly)
Ahh.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.400
The Detroit news is reporting something

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.150
that sparked my interest.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:44.490
Lego is going to swap
out its plastic packaging

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.759
in favor of paper packaging!

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:48.468
(audience applauds)

00:03:48.468 --> 00:03:51.580
And all because kids demanded it.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.810
However, Lego pieces are
still incredibly painful

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.007
to step on and maybe they
can work on that next.

00:03:57.007 --> 00:03:57.840
(audience laughs)

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.200
These kids make me feel so optimistic.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.870
I mean, Lego is a huge brand.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:06.070
This will have a giant impact.

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:09.000
Reducing plastic is so important.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.980
My friend, Cameron Diaz,
I'm not name-dropping,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.498
she just happens to be my friend.

00:04:12.498 --> 00:04:13.400
(audience laughs)

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.860
She used to get on me
for the last 20 years,

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:17.190
she was so ahead of the curve.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.760
You know, she's always been
environmentally conscious

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:21.320
and she used to turn off all my lights

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:22.310
and my pool heater

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:25.860
and tell me to go get a
hybrid car, and you know?

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:27.630
I finally, I switched.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:29.220
I made her a promise a year ago

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:33.130
and I drink out of these
paper water companies

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:35.640
which they show that the
packaging is possible

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.170
and they taste delicious.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:38.280
I really love them.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.500
And I have this metal cup that's missing.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:41.870
I'll show you later.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:44.150
And I used to drink out of styrofoam

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.750
so like this is a big deal for me.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.670
And I'm one small person,

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.400
I don't think I can make a big change.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.080
And when I see something
like Lego is doing this

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.700
then I think change is possible.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.200
And if we all continue to be
mindful about these things,

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.160
we can change our world for our kids.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.810
But again, parents, when you're screaming

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.178
after stepping on one of those Legos.

00:05:05.178 --> 00:05:07.030
Ahh!
(audience laughs)

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:07.893
That's okay too.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.450
So moving on.
(audience applauds)

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:11.450
Our next story.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.250
'Cause that's what happens
when you step on a Lego. Ahh!

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.010
This story warmed my heart.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.420
In a radio interview the other day,

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:22.080
Usher shared this story behind
his latest single, "I Cry."

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.190
And he's quoted as saying that

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.730
he didn't have a
relationship with his father.

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.710
And so when he was watching a
movie with his two young sons

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:33.510
and there was an emotional
father/son moment on screen,

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.250
he started to cry.

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:37.827
And then his sons were
shocked and they asked him,

00:05:37.827 --> 00:05:39.220
"Are you crying, Dad?"

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:42.087
And he said that he
took that moment to say,

00:05:42.087 --> 00:05:44.990
"Yeah, I am crying," and that it was okay.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:47.270
And a song was born.

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:48.103
Thank you, Usher.

00:05:48.103 --> 00:05:49.850
We should be teaching boys

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:52.320
that it's okay to cry at an early age.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.160
And he says that empathy can
bring us all closer together,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.840
incredibly difficult
times such as we're in,

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.340
and I couldn't agree more.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:03.000
So it turns out, men are
crying now more than ever.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.173
Welcome to the club.

00:06:05.107 --> 00:06:08.190
(upbeat dance music)

